  comment   = {The study investigates whether gaze reveals the cognitive and motor processes underlying skill and thus enables more accurate assessment. SkillSight is presented as a two-stage multimodal framework for first-person data. SkillSight-T (teacher) integrates egocentric video and gaze to learn skill-related features that generalize across in-the-wild scenes by modeling gaze--action interactions, object fixations and transitions, gaze-cropped image encodings, and dynamic gaze patterns. SkillSight-S (student) relies only on gaze (camera off) to reduce power use and improve privacy; it is trained via knowledge distillation to transfer visual knowledge from SkillSight-T into gaze representations. Evaluation is performed on three datasets (EgoExo4D, Multisense Badminton, Expert-Novice Soccer) covering cooking, music, and sports. Quantitative and qualitative analyses demonstrate when and how gaze reflects skill and how predictions align with psychological theories.}
}

